# Revision History # {#revision-history}

## R3 (2026-10-12) ## {#revision-r3}

- Update the proposal for the C++26 draft N5050.
- Account for type and value pack indexing without changing which expansion handles an existing pack occurrence.
- Distinguish a new homogeneous parameter pack from an ordinary expansion of an enclosing pack.
- Narrow the new lambda genericness rule to homogeneous declarations.
- Separate the preferred comma-less varargs reclamation from the compatibility decision it requires.
- Defer explicit lengths.

## R2 (2019-10-05) ## {#revision-r2}

- Rebased wording against N4830.
- Added discussion on the impact of making varargs commas mandatory.

## R1 (2019-03-11) ## {#revision-r1}

- Rebased wording against N4800.
- Added wording for signature changes.
- Removed wording permitting empty template parameter lists for template declarations.
- Added feature test macro.
- Added discussion of alternative designs aimed at addressing or avoiding confusion with explicit specializations.
- Added straw polls from EWGI in Kona.
- Added acknowledgments.
- Minor corrections to examples.

## R0 (2018-10-08) ## {#revision-r0}

- Initial draft. [[P1219R2]]

# Introduction # {#introduction}

This paper proposes function parameter packs with a single element type. If the committee permits us to reclaim the comma-less syntax, we could use the following form:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T> // T is not a parameter pack...
void foo(T... vs); // ...but vs is a parameter pack.
</xmp>

C++ already permits a homogeneous template parameter pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Legal since C++11: T is not a parameter pack, but vs is.
template <class T, T... vs>
void foo();
</xmp>

The first pack would contain ordinary function parameters initialized at the call. The second contains constant template arguments. [[CXX-VARIADIC]] A homogeneous function parameter pack deduces its length at compile time while accepting values computed at runtime.

This revision proposes a single homogeneous pack at the end of a function's parameter list. The pack could be named or unnamed. The function would implicitly be a template and its specializations would be distinguished in part by the pack length. The named spelling is unambiguous. The unnamed spelling <code>int...</code> requires committee approval of the comma-less C-varargs change in [[#compatibility]]. The examples and wording below use this syntax subject to that approval.

1. A declarator ellipsis introduces a new homogeneous pack only when its parameter pattern has no remaining unexpanded pack after ordinary placeholder processing.
2. Existing indexed selection and nested expansions retain their meanings, including in lambdas.
3. Calls, target function types, and declarations identifying particular specializations deduce the implicit length. Ordinary redeclarations preserve the same homogeneous declaration.

