# Definitions # {#definitions}

A <em>homogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>homogeneous pack</em>) contains values of one type and its declaration introduces an independent count. A <em>heterogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>heterogeneous pack</em>) permits values of different types.

These terms apply to both function parameter packs and template parameter packs.

A homogeneous function parameter pack's parameter pattern contains no unexpanded pack after placeholder processing. An ordinary expansion remains an ordinary expansion even when every resulting parameter type is the same.

# Implementation Experience # {#implementation}

An implementation of the R2 proposal based on Clang can be found in <a href="https://github.com/Bekenn/clang/tree/func-parm-packs">the prototype repository</a>. [[P1219R2]] [[P1219-CLANG]]

<aside class=issue>
<p>Update the R2 prototype to a current Clang release and test this revision in modern C++ language modes.</p>
</aside>

# Motivation and Scope # {#motivation}
## Meeting user expectations ## {#symmetry}
<p>This table shows the current lack of symmetry between template parameter packs and function parameter packs: [[CXX-VARIADIC]] [[CXX-AUTO]]</p>
<table class=data>
<caption>Parameter-pack declarations in C++26. Each declaration is independent, and the ill-formed function-parameter form is proposed.</caption>
    <tr><th></th><th>Template parameter pack</th><th>Function parameter pack</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Heterogeneous</th>
        <td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <auto... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
void f(auto... vs);
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Homogeneous</th>
        <td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <class Type, Type... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td class=code-invalid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Ill-formed:
template <class Type>
void f(Type... vs);
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## Greater expressivity ## {#expressivity}

<p>R2 cited eight <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/">Stack Overflow</a> questions asking how to write a function with a homogeneous parameter pack. [[SO-1]] [[SO-2]] [[SO-3]] [[SO-4]] [[SO-5]] [[SO-6]] [[SO-7]] [[SO-8]]</p>

<p>In May 2022, a programmer asked for a fixed parameter type with ordinary implicit conversions and explained why constraining deduced argument types with <code>std::same_as</code> or <code>std::convertible_to</code> does not provide that behavior. [[SO-9]]
In January 2026, another programmer asked how to declare a function taking an even number of <code>int</code> arguments and was unsure why templates were needed when all parameters had a concrete type. [[SO-10]]</p>

<p>Each workaround below is independent. The homogeneous-pack row is proposed.</p>
<table class=data>
<caption>Workarounds and the proposed homogeneous-pack form, with complete function bodies.</caption>
    <tr>
        <th>Recursion</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T min(T v)
{
    return v;
}

template <class T, class... Args>
T min(T v1, Args... vs)
{
    T v2 = min(vs...);
    return v2 < v1 ? v2 : v1;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th><code>std::initializer_list</code></th>
        <td>
<p>Precondition: <code>vs.size() &gt; 0</code>.</p>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T min(std::initializer_list<T> vs)
{
    auto i = vs.begin();
    T v = *i++;
    for (; i != vs.end(); ++i)
    {
        if (*i < v)
            v = *i;
    }

    return v;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>SFINAE</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T, class... Args,
          std::enable_if_t<(... && std::is_same_v<Args, T>),
                           std::nullptr_t> = nullptr>
T min(T v1, Args... vs)
{
    return (v1, ..., (v1 = vs < v1 ? vs : v1));
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Concepts</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T, class... Args>
    requires (... && std::same_as<Args, T>)
T min(T v1, Args... vs)
{
    return (v1, ..., (v1 = vs < v1 ? vs : v1));
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Homogeneous pack (this proposal)</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T min(T v1, T... vs)
{
    return (v1, ..., (v1 = vs < v1 ? vs : v1));
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p>The work-arounds suffer from a lack of clarity in the interface.</p>
<ul>
<li>The recursion approach advertises a function that can take variadic arguments of any type, but any attempt to call the function with a non-<code>T</code> will either cause the program to fail to compile, or lead to potentially unwanted implicit conversions.</li>
<li>The <code>std::initializer_list</code> approach correctly advertises the accepted type, but is inflexible with respect to mutability and requires the user to enclose its arguments in braces.
In the <code>min</code> example above, the precondition could be removed if an explicit argument preceded the <code>std::initializer_list</code>, but the syntactic separation remains, resulting in calls such as <code>min(a, { b, c, d })</code>.</li>
<li>The SFINAE approach achieves the goal of constraining all arguments to be of the same type, but at the expense of readability, and the application of the <code>is_same_v</code> trait can disable desired conversions when constraining to a non-dependent parameter type.</li>
<li>The Concepts approach is more readable than the SFINAE approach, but is otherwise interchangeable.</li>
</ul>

<p>Explicitly specifying <code>T</code> also permits ordinary argument conversions in the proposed form. The SFINAE and Concepts forms still deduce each <code>Args</code> and reject a different type. For example, their <code>min&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code> calls fail the equal-type requirement. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]] [[CXX-EXPLICIT-ARGS]]</p>

<p>If the committee permits us to reclaim the comma-less syntax, we could use this form for a fixed element type without an explicit template head. Each argument would initialize an <code>int</code> parameter:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}

int use_sum(int runtime_value) {
    return sum(runtime_value, 2.5);
}
</xmp>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Develop and test braced-initializer calls for a known aggregate type without CTAD or repeated argument types. Compare constrained packs and initializer lists, covering deduction, empty calls and initializers, designated initializers, and narrowing. Unnamed packs require committee approval to reclaim the comma-less syntax. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.aggr">[dcl.init.aggr]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a></p>
</aside>

<p>The earlier workarounds can be found in the Library Fundamentals TS v2 in the form of <code>make_array</code>, and even in the standard for <code>std::min</code> and <code>std::max</code> and the deduction guide for <code>std::array</code>.
All of these could arguably be written more naturally with homogeneous parameter packs. [[LFTS2-ARRAY-CREATION]] [[CXX-MIN-MAX]] [[CXX-ARRAY-DEDUCTION]]</p>

<p>The library declarations below omit their enclosing namespace. In the TS declaration, <code>VT</code> is <code>common_type_t&lt;Types...&gt;</code> when <code>D</code> is <code>void</code>, and is <code>D</code> otherwise. The current <code>std::array</code> deduction guide has the mandate that <code>(is_same_v&lt;T, U&gt; &amp;&amp; ...)</code> is true. [[LFTS2-ARRAY-CREATION]] [[CXX-ARRAY-DEDUCTION]]</p>

<table class=data>
<caption>Standard and Library Fundamentals TS v2 interfaces compared with proposed homogeneous-pack interfaces.</caption>
    <tr><th>Current</th><th>With homogeneous packs</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
constexpr T min(initializer_list<T> t);

template <class T, class Compare>
constexpr T min(initializer_list<T> t, Compare comp);
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
constexpr T min(T v1, T... vs);

template <class Compare, class T>
constexpr T min(Compare comp, T v1, T... vs);
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class D = void, class... Types>
constexpr array<VT, sizeof...(Types)>
    make_array(Types&&... t);
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
constexpr auto make_array(T... t)
    -> array<T, sizeof...(t)>;
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template<class T, class... U>
array(T, U...) -> array<T, 1 + sizeof...(U)>;
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template<class T>
array(T... t) -> array<T, sizeof...(t)>;
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p>The proposed <code>make_array</code> is not a replacement with identical deduction semantics. The TS can find a common type for mixed arguments when <code>D</code> is omitted. The proposed <code>T... t</code> uses ordinary deduction for one <code>T</code>, so inconsistent deductions fail. Explicitly supplying <code>T</code> permits ordinary parameter conversions. An empty call cannot deduce <code>T</code>, but an explicitly supplied <code>T</code> permits an empty pack. The TS also permits an empty call when <code>D</code> is supplied. [[LFTS2-ARRAY-CREATION]] [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]] [[CXX-EXPLICIT-ARGS]]</p>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Decide whether to support homogeneous packs in deduction guides and, if so, specify deduction and return-type rules.</p>
</aside>

## Initializer lists ## {#initializer-lists}

<table class=data>
<caption>Homogeneous function parameter packs compared with std::initializer_list.</caption>
    <tr><th></th><th><code>std::initializer_list</code></th><th>homogeneous packs</th></tr>
    <tr><th>Can be used outside of a template?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Can mutate elements?</th><td>No</td><td>Yes, when the declared element type permits it</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Can appear before other arguments?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Get the number of elements</th><td><code class="prettyprint">x.size()</code></td><td><code class="prettyprint">sizeof...(x)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><th>Iterate over the elements</th><td>Range-based <code class="prettyprint">for</code></td><td>Fold expressions</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Homogeneous function parameter packs are <em>not</em> intended as a replacement for <code>std::initializer_list</code>.
A <code>std::initializer_list</code> provides access to an array of const elements and is passed as a single argument. A function parameter pack accepts separate arguments. [[CXX-INITIALIZER-LIST]] [[CXX-VARIADIC]]
List-initialization gives initializer-list constructors priority under [over.match.list], which can prevent selection of a constructor taking a parameter pack. [[CXX-LIST-OVERLOAD]]
Consider the case of container initialization:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer
{
public:
    MyContainer(std::initializer_list<T> elems);
    // ...
};
</xmp>

<p>List-initialization selects this constructor:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
MyContainer<int> cont = { 5, 10 };  // invokes MyContainer<int>::MyContainer(std::initializer_list<int>)
</xmp>

<p>The corresponding homogeneous constructor would permit the same initialization:</p>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Decide whether to permit homogeneous packs in constructors and test the examples under R2's overload model, treating the constructor as an implicit template.</p>
</aside>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer
{
public:
    MyContainer(const T&... elems);
    // ...
};

// invokes MyContainer<int>::MyContainer(const int&, const int&)
MyContainer<int> cont = { 5, 10 };  
</xmp>

<p><em>...unless</em> there is a competing constructor that is a better match:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer
{
public:
    MyContainer(const T&... elems);
    explicit MyContainer(const T& min, const T& max);
    // ...
};

// error: MyContainer<int>::MyContainer(const int&, const int&) is explicit
MyContainer<int> cont = { 5, 10 };  
</xmp>

<p>With the additional constructor, it is now impossible to invoke the constructor containing the homogeneous parameter pack when there are exactly two elements, regardless of the initialization syntax chosen.
In contrast, the constructor with <code>std::initializer_list</code> is chosen when using list-initialization in these examples, and can be unambiguously selected (or not) when using direct non-list initialization.</p>

<table class="data constructor-comparison">
<caption>Constructor selection for the listed declarations. The homogeneous-pack column gives proposed outcomes.</caption>
<colgroup><col style="width: 20%"><col style="width: 40%"><col style="width: 40%"></colgroup>
<thead>
    <tr><th scope=col>Declaration</th><th scope=col>With <code>std::initializer_list</code></th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
    <tr>
        <td></td>
        <td><xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer
{
public:
    MyContainer(
        std::initializer_list<T> elems);
    explicit MyContainer(
        const T& min, const T& max);
    // ...
};
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td><xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer
{
public:
    MyContainer(
        const T&... elems);
    explicit MyContainer(
        const T& min, const T& max);
    // ...
};
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont{1, 2, 3};</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont = { 1, 2, 3 };</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont(1, 2, 3);</code></td>
        <td>Error: no matching constructor</td>
        <td>Selects homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont({ 1, 2, 3 });</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Error: no matching constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont{1, 2};</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont = { 1, 2 };</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Error: selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont(1, 2);</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont({ 1, 2 });</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Error: no matching constructor</td>
    </tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>For this reason, homogeneous packs are likely inappropriate for constructors.
Homogeneous packs are likely the better option outside of constructors, where initializer lists must be separately enclosed in curly braces.
One possible exception to this is when the API designer wishes to <em>move</em> or <em>forward</em> variadic constructor arguments rather than <em>copy</em> them.
Frustratingly, the <code>std::initializer_list</code> template permits access to its elements only via references to <code>const</code>, whereas packs do not have this limitation. [[CXX-INITIALIZER-LIST]]</p>


<aside class=issue>
<p>Agree with Jan and Eczbek which maintained interface to use as an example. Compare homogeneous packs with constrained packs, initializer lists, ranges, and bounded overloads.</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Compare typed runtime variadics and checked C varargs, including D's variadics and GCC's argument-count builtin. Distinguish count from type checks and account for default promotions. <a href="https://dlang.org/spec/function.html#typesafe_variadic_functions">D variadics</a> <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Constructing-Calls.html">GCC argument-count builtin</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call">[expr.call]</a></p>
</aside>

